1 DEBUG

   Starts the debugger on the virtual VAX as it stands, with nothing
   running: the way to examine memory and the registers, or set a
   breakpoint, before running a program (as Ctrl/Y and then DEBUG does
   for a VMS process).

   Format

     DEBUG

2 Description

   The prompt changes to DBG>.  The debugger is a command set of its
   own, modeled on the VMS debugger: EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, STEP, GO, SET
   BREAK, SHOW CALLS, and so on, with HELP at DBG> for each.  The
   console's own commands (DIRECTORY, MACRO, LINK, RUN, ...) aren't
   available there.  EXIT returns to the console, leaving the machine as
   it is.

   GO, CALL, and ASM (the assembler that puts code into memory) are the
   debugger's commands, for working with live code.  The console's ASM
   is accepted only until the microkernel is in place, which is how
   STARTUP.COM loads it.

   The debugger also starts by itself when a program stops: a GO or
   CALL that reaches a breakpoint, RUN/DEBUG, or an image linked /DEBUG.
   A program that runs to its end returns to the console prompt.

2 Examples

   1.$ DEBUG
     DBG> EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION EXE$INITIALIZE
     DBG> EXIT

     Looks at the microkernel's first instruction.
